{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/7/prompt.md | sed -n '1,220p'\"","aggregated_output":"1:你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n2:\n3:每个参考题描述包含：\n4:- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n5:- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n6:- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n7:- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n8:\n9:参考题目如下：\n10:<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7666fa5d\" lines=\"12-26\">\n11:<description>\n12:Fill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.\n13:\n14:Input structure:\n15:- bg = most common color.\n16:- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).\n17:- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.\n18:\n19:Rule:\n20:- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.\n21:- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).\n22:- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).\n23:\n24:Key insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.\n25:</description>\n26:</reference_puzzle>\n27:\n28:<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n29:<description>\n30:Grow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n31:\n32:Input structure:\n33:- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n34:\n35:Rule:\n36:- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n37:- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n38:- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n39:- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n40:\n41:Key insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n42:</description>\n43:</reference_puzzle>\n44:\n45:<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fbf15a0b\" lines=\"49-69\">\n46:<description>\n47:Crop out the grid half containing the two aligned marker pixels, then erase the markers to background.\n48:\n49:Input structure\n50:- The grid has a dominant background color and one non-background marker color\n51:  that appears as exactly two cells.\n52:- The marker cells are aligned either in one row or one column and both lie\n53:  in the same half along that direction.\n54:- Other non-background pixels are content inside one or both halves.\n55:\n56:Rule\n57:- If the markers share a row, keep the left or right half whose columns\n58:  contain them.\n59:- If the markers share a column, keep the top or bottom half whose rows\n60:  contain them.\n61:- In the kept half, recolor the marker pixels to the background color.\n62:\n63:Key insight\n64:- The two marker pixels are not part of the picture; their alignment and\n65:  half-position identify which half of the doubled canvas is the answer.\n66:</description>\n67:</reference_puzzle>\n68:\n69:<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b71a7747\" lines=\"10-23\">\n70:<description>\n71:Extract the hidden two-color image by compacting the rows and columns of its complete mask.\n72:\n73:Input structure\n74:The grid contains one pair of colors whose pixels occupy every cell in the\n75:cross product of their active rows and active columns; all other colors form\n76:the surrounding filler or separators.\n77:Rule\n78:Find that unique color pair, keep exactly the rows and columns touched by\n79:the pair, and return the induced subgrid.\n80:Key insight\n81:The target image is not chosen by color id; it is the only two-color mask\n82:whose occupied pixels form a full rectangular row/column product after\n83:deleting unused rows and columns.\n84:</description>\n85:</reference_puzzle>\n86:\n87:<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"15660dd6\" lines=\"33-54\">\n88:<description>\n89:Combine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.\n90:\n91:Input structure:\n92:- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.\n93:- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.\n94:- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,\n95:  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on\n96:  the background.\n97:\n98:Rule:\n99:- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.\n100:- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.\n101:- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask\n102:  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one\n103:  background column.\n104:\n105:Key insight:\n106:The table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies\n107:the paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply\n108:the shape mask.\n109:</description>\n110:</reference_puzzle>\n111:\n112:<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n113:<description>\n114:Split the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n115:\n116:Input structure:\n117:- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n118:- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n119:- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n120:\n121:Rule:\n122:- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n123:- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n124:- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n125:  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n126:\n127:Key insight:\n128:- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n129:  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n130:</description>\n131:</reference_puzzle>\n132:\n133:<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n134:<description>\n135:Recover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n136:\n137:Input structure:\n138:- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n139:- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n140:\n141:Rule:\n142:- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n143:- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n144:- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n145:\n146:Key insight:\n147:The hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n148:</description>\n149:</reference_puzzle>\n150:\n151:<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7d7772cc\" lines=\"70-92\">\n152:<description>\n153:Move each source pixel toward the separator when it matches its aligned reference pixel, otherwise to the far edge.\n154:\n155:Input structure:\n156:- A single full separator line is part of a rectangular frame; the frame's\n157:  extra cells mark the reference side.\n158:- On the reference side, one row or column of colored pixels is aligned\n159:  with one row or column of source pixels on the opposite side.\n160:- Each side has its own dominant background color.\n161:\n162:Rule:\n163:- Compare each source pixel with the reference pixel on the same row or column.\n164:- If their colors match, place the source pixel in the cell adjacent to the\n165:  separator on the source side.\n166:- If their colors differ, place the source pixel on the far outer edge along\n167:  the same row or column.\n168:- Erase the original source pixels to the source-side background; keep the\n169:  frame and reference pixels unchanged.\n170:\n171:Key insight:\n172:The extra frame cells identify which side is the reference; equality with the\n173:aligned reference pixel decides whether each source pixel moves inward or outward.\n174:</description>\n175:</reference_puzzle>\n176:\n177:<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"48634b99\" lines=\"14-27\">\n178:<description>\n179:Advance the marker from one half of a vertical bar to the same half of the next longer bar.\n180:\n181:Input structure\n182:- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n183:- One foreground color forms many vertical one-cell-wide bars; the other appears as a single vertical marker segment.\n184:- The marker segment occupies exactly the top or bottom half of its containing vertical bar.\n185:Rule\n186:- Recolor the current marker segment back to the bar color.\n187:- Find the unique all-bar-color vertical bar whose height is two pixels greater than the marked source bar.\n188:- Recolor the same half of that next longer bar to the marker color.\n189:Key insight\n190:- Each bar's total height encodes an ordered size, and the marker advances from a 2k bar to the corresponding 2(k+1) bar while preserving whether it marks the top or bottom half.\n191:</description>\n192:</reference_puzzle>\n193:\n194:<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a416fc5b\" lines=\"95-106\">\n195:<description>\n196:Add the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.\n197:\n198:Input structure\n199:An 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.\n200:\n201:Rule\n202:With only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.\n203:\n204:Key insight\n205:The non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.\n206:</description>\n207:</reference_puzzle>\n208:\n209:<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a47bf94d\" lines=\"178-189\">\n210:<description>\n211:Transmit colored tokens along wire networks, converting each to its complementary 3×3 pattern.\n212:\n213:Input structure:\n214:- bg = background. Non-bg cells form wire networks and 3×3 tokens (solid blocks or X/diagonal patterns) near wire endpoints.\n215:\n216:Rule:\n217:- Every solid 3×3 token is replaced in-place by a diamond (center + 4 cardinal neighbors).\n218:- Each token near a wire endpoint sends its complement to the opposite endpoint: solid emits X, X emits diamond.\n219:\n220:Key insight: Token shape cycles solid → X → diamond; wires route each complement to the far end.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
